      "technical_explanation": "The static analyzer flagged two address-like string literals on the cited line under a network-access 'shady-link' rule. In the supplied source, that line sits inside the export-masking routine: the two literals are the loopback IPv4 address and the unspecified-any IPv4 address, used solely in equality comparisons so those universally identical values are left unmasked when a user exports console logs, while other machine-specific addresses are replaced. The literals are never used to construct a request, fetch, or any other network destination; no outbound data flow appears in the supplied evidence. The extension's only disclosed remote loading is the Eruda library from a public CDN when the user enables the console, which matches its stated purpose and is user-initiated. The flagged signal is a scanner false positive on benign comparison literals, not demonstrated exposure.",
      "layman_explanation": "The scanner saw two text values that look like web addresses and raised a flag. They are actually two well-known addresses that mean 'this same computer' and 'any address'—identical on every machine, so they are not private. The extension keeps a list of them so its log-sharing feature does not need to hide them. Nothing is sent anywhere: the flagged values are only compared against other text to decide what to mask before a user shares an exported log.",
